<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>The system prompt of a large language model (LLM) defines the model's role,
guardrails, and instructions, and is normally trusted more highly than user
input. If untrusted data is used to build the system prompt, an attacker may be
able to override those instructions, disable safety measures, or otherwise
manipulate the model's behavior. This is a form of prompt injection
(CWE-1427).</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>Keep the system prompt under application control and never build it from
untrusted input. Place untrusted data in a user- or human-role message instead
of the system message, so the model treats it as data rather than as
instructions. If untrusted values must influence the system prompt, constrain
them to a fixed allowlist of trusted strings.</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>In the following example, untrusted input taken from an HTTP request is used
directly as the content of a LangChainGo <code>SystemChatMessage</code>. An
attacker can supply text that overrides the intended instructions of the
model.</p>

<sample src="example/example_bad.go" />

<p>In the following example, the system prompt is a fixed, trusted string and the
untrusted input is confined to a <code>HumanChatMessage</code>, so it is treated
as data rather than as instructions.</p>

<sample src="example/example_good.go" />
</example>

<references>
<li>OWASP: <a href="https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/">LLM01: Prompt Injection</a>.</li>
<li>MITRE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1427.html">CWE-1427: Improper Neutralization of Input Used for LLM Prompting</a>.</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
